A||||1||JohnnyK||Lock rebuilding...||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||11-30-2000||01:27 PM||||As someone just brought this up, I need to know how to rebuild a lock cylinder (or get it out).. The key will not go in at all on the drivers side. Perhaps something is broken off in there? If so, is there a way to get it out without going through all this needless work? THanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||11-30-2000||02:59 PM||||You'll need to pull the interior door panel.  Takes only a few minutes.<P>Unhook the linkage to the arm on the lock cylinder, by releasing a tab, and sliding the clip, be careful not to drop it.  Use a screwdriver or pliers to pull the clip that holds the lock cylinder to the door reinforcement, be sure that the clip doesn't drop down in the door also, use tape or something to hold it while you have the lock out.<P>You may want to try to repair, clean, or rebuild the lock cylinder.  Replacements are fairly inexpensive and can be ordered through a Pontiac dealer.  To transfer the "tumblers" from the old cylinder to the new one.  Carefully remove the sheet metal cover on the side of the cylinder that holds them in, and note and keep track of the "tumbers" in order and direction they came out.  Drop them in the new cylinder, put the new springs in, and press the metal cover back on.  Check to make sure the cylinder works with the key.<P>Putting the cylinder back in the door and getting the clip to hold it in right may take a few tries (Probably the hardest part of the job).  Hook up the linkage and put the door pad back on.  Have any questions, feel free to post em.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-30-2000).]||||reg||1
